"d":477},{"n":"ΠΑΠΑΔΑΚΗ ΑΘΑΝΑΣΙΑ","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":36.893017,"lo":27.293987,"d":485,"h":"Mo-Fr 08:30-21:00; Sa 09:00-20:00"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":36.89301,"lo":27.293985,"d":485},{"n":"Rabbit","g":"essentials","k":"mobile_phone","la":36.892744,"lo":27.285914,"d":512},{"n":"Happy Wash Laundry","g":"essentials","k":"laundry","la":36.89135,"lo":27.291109,"d":526,"h":"Mo-Sa 09:00-21:00","ph":"+302242023424"},{"n":"ΓΟΥΝΑΡΗΣ ΕΜΜΑΝΟΥΗΛ","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":36.891918,"lo":27.293005,"d":526,"h":"Sa 09:30-14:00; Mo,Tu,Th,Fr 08:30-14:00,16:30-20:30; We 08:30-14:00","ph":"+30 2242 026611"},{"n":"Bab Gedid Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.891111,"lo":27.286111,"d":644,"ds":"The Bab Gedid Mosque, also known as the Eski Mosque (Greek: Εσκί Τζαμί, from Turkish: Eski Camii, meaning \"the old mosque\") was an Ottoman-period mosque on the Aegean island of Kos, in southeastern Greece, before it was badly damaged in an earthquake in 1933 and finally demolished a few years later in 1935. Today its octagonal stone minaret is all that survives from the original structure.","wpu":"Bab Gedid Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Kos-Kos_city-Bab_Gedid_Mosque_or_Eski-13ESD.jpg/960px-Kos-Kos_city-Bab_Gedid_Mosque_or_Eski-13ESD.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Temple of Asclepius, Kos","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.8757,"lo":27.2571,"d":3695,"ds":"The Temple of Asclepius or Asclepieion of Kos was one of the most famous temples dedicated to Asclepius in the ancient world. The archeologist Rudolf Herzog, grandfather of Werner Herzog, excavated it in 1902, as the latter recounted in conversation with Paul Holdengräber.","wpu":"Temple of Asclepius, Kos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Kos_museum_mos01.JPG/960px-Kos_museum_mos01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kargı Adası","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.9515,"lo":27.305242,"d":6318,"ds":"Kargı Adası is an uninhabited Turkish islet in the Aegean Sea, in Muğla Province, and a tourist destination renowned for fishing and scuba diving. It is likely that the island takes its name, which is literally translated as Spear Island, from the rock formations that make up the islet and resemble spears pointed towards the sky.","wpu":"Kargı Adası","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Karg%C4%B1_Adas%C4%B1_Kayal%C4%B1klar_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Karg%C4%B1_Adas%C4%B1_Kayal%C4%B1klar_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dikaios, Kos","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.866667,"lo":27.2,"d":8643,"ds":"Dikaios (Greek: Δίκαιος) is a former municipality on the island of Kos, in the Dodecanese, Greece. Since the 2011 local government reform, it is part of the municipality Kos, of which it is a municipal unit. Population 8,192 (2021).","wpu":"Dikaios, Kos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/DE_Dikeou.svg/960px-DE_Dikeou.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Kos or Cos (; Greek: Κως [kos]) is a Greek island, part of the Dodecanese island chain in the southeastern Aegean Sea. Kos is the third largest island of the Dodecanese, after Rhodes and Karpathos; it has a population of 37,089 (2021 census), making it the second most populous of the Dodecanese after Rhodes. The island measures 42.1 by 11.5 kilometres (26 by 7 miles).","wpu":"Kos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Greece_kos_city.jpg/960px-Greece_kos_city.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}